{{History of Balisca}}
Balisca participated in the [[Great Adonian War]] from 12 November 1939 to 2 September 1945, fighting as a member of the [[W:Allies of World War II|Allied Powers]] against the [[W:Axis powers of World War II|Axis Powers]]. The nation's entry into the global conflict marked a definitive end to its period of armed neutrality and solidified its [[W:Anti-fascism|anti-fascist]] and [[W:Internationalism (politics)|pro-internationalist]] stance. Balisca's role extended across multiple theaters, notably contributing to naval operations in the [[W:Battle of the Atlantic|Battle of the Atlantic]], ground campaigns in [[W:North African campaign|North Africa]] and [[W:Italian campaign (World War II)|Italy]], and supporting the [[W:Liberation of France|Liberation of France]] and [[Philippines#Japanese occupation of the Philippines (1942 - 1945)|the Philippines]].

== Background ==
{{Main|Estau Nuevo}}
The 1930s in Balisca, known as the [[Estau Nuevo|''Estau Nuevo'']] ("New State") period, were characterized by a strategic centralization of certain political and economic powers to address severe global challenges. Balisca's economy, guided by the [[Instituto por la Reconstrución Industrial|IRI]] (established 1933), exhibited resilience during the [[W:Great Depression|Great Depression]] and saw a concurrent increase in industrial production, supporting early rearmament efforts.

Even before the formal declaration of war, the confederal government had expressed an openly hostile stance towards the expansionist fascist nations. Reflecting its "pro-internationalist diplomatic orientation", Balisca joined the [[W:League of Nations|League of Nations]]' economic blockades against [[W:Fascist Italy|Italy]] following its invasion and annexation of the [[W:Ethiopian Empire|Ethiopian Empire]] in 1935. In 1936 it condemned the Empire of Japan following it's [[W:Second Sino-Japanese War|invasion of the Republic of China]]. A few years later, in March 1938, Balisca officially protested before the League of Nations against the violation of [[W:Austria|Austrian]] sovereignty after the [[W:Anschluss|Anschluss]]. Further demonstrating its anti-fascist commitment, the government turned a blind eye to the [[Baliscan Mafia|Gardunya]] and [[International Brigades|Internationalist Brigades]] directly transporting munitions and fighters to the [[W:Spanish Republican forces|Spanish Republican forces]] during the [[W:Spanish Civil War|Spanish Civil War]]. Following the victory of Nationalists forces, Balisca removed its diplomatic staff from Nationalist Spain and maintained formal recognition of the [[W:Spanish Republican government in exile|Spanish Republican government in exile]]. Concurrently, Balisca showcased its humanitarian principles by welcoming around 200,000 Jews from Nazi Germany and German-occupied Europe by 1939, at a time when many other American countries refused them entry.

=== Rearmament and mobilization ===
The defeat of Republican forces in the Spanish Civil War underscored the growing threat of fascism in Europe. This fueled widespread anxiety among Baliscan citizens and the military, leading to increased calls for confederal re-armament. These concerns culminated in the calling of the [[1939 Confederal Convention]], a pivotal event that solidified national resolve and authorized the necessary shift towards wartime preparedness. The Estau Nuevo's consolidation of economic power through entities like the IRI provided the means for this rapid mobilization and rearmament.

== Aftermath and legacy ==
{{Main|Baliscan miracle}}The conclusion of the war in 1947 marked a profound shift in Balisca's standing on the international stage. The war effort, particularly the significant contributions of the Baliscan Expeditionary Force and its naval assets across multiple theaters, solidified Balisca's reputation as a formidable military power and a steadfast anti-fascist force. The unity forged during the conflict, along with the industrial expansion under the IRI, laid the groundwork for the ensuing economic boom. This period saw rapid infrastructure expansion, technological advancement, and a substantial improvement in the quality of life for broad swathes of society.

However, Balisca's significant contributions to the Allied victory did not translate into an equivalent seat at the table in the shaping of the post-war global order. Despite its substantial sacrifice and its ideological commitment to internationalism, Balisca was conspicuously absent from the high-level conferences that established the [[W:United Nations|United Nations]]. Delegations from the Allied "[[W:Four Policemen|Big Four]]" (the Soviet Union, the UK, the US, and the [[W:Republic of China (1912–1949)|Republic of China]]) – and later France as the "Big Five" – met at conferences such as [[W:Dumbarton Oaks Conference|Dumbarton Oaks]] and [[W:Yalta Conference|Yalta]] to negotiate the UN's structure, including the composition of the powerful [[W:UN Security Council|UN Security Council]].

Balisca's exclusion from the permanent membership of the Security Council was not due to a lack of military contribution or geopolitical importance, but a direct consequence of its radical libertarian socialist and anarchist orientation. The very structure of Balisca was seen as an ideological threat to the world order the Great Powers sought to build. To the capitalist West, Balisca was labelled as "communist-adjacent", and was seen as a dangerous alternative to be contained as the [[W:Cold War|Cold War]] began to take shape. To the Soviet Union, its "stateless socialism" was a [[W:Revisionism (Marxism)|heterodox]], [[W:Counter-revolution|counter-revolutionary]] deviation from their own authoritarian model. This fundamental opposition was rooted in how Balisca's emphasis on communal power, cantonal autonomy, and the [[W:Right to resist|right to resist]] challenged the [[W: Westphalian system|Westphalian notion of centralized state sovereignty]] that the UN was designed to uphold. This ideological mistrust was compounded by pragmatic concerns, as the major powers doubted if Balisca's fluid, consensus-driven system could reliably commit to or enforce binding international resolutions, perceiving its radical democracy as a source of unpredictable instability.

Consequently, Balisca, despite its moral clarity and battlefield prowess, was deliberately sidelined by the emerging global powers. This experience reinforced the long-standing societal skepticism of alliances and the [[W:Great powers|great powers]], solidifying Balisca's shift towards the [[W:Non-Aligned Movement|Non-Aligned Movement]].

